Separate the Type Parameter Declaration from the function signature and the Type Parameter usage while maintaining the order of declaration.
This will be clearer to diff from the non-generics old code when comparing.
This will also be apparent that this is actually the type's parameter because the declaration is literally beside the `type` or `func`

The Type Parameter should be placed after the keyword `type` or `func` with no space in between.
This will make the type signature still be clear from being confused from the long paired symbols like parenthesis and square brackets.

```go
// non type parameter
type M []int

// before
type M[T any] []T

// after
// declaration usage
//   v          v
type[T any] M []T

// non type parameter
func Foo(t T) (val T, err error) {
	...
}

// before
//      declaration & usage
//      v~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~v
func Foo[T any](t T) (val T, err error) {
	...
}

// after
// declaration usage
//  v~~~~~v    v~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~v
func[T any] Foo(t T) (val T, err error) {
	...
}

```

The Type Parameter should be also placed before the method receiver signature.
This will make clear that the Type Parameter declaration can be used by any type toward the right with the emphasis on the method receiver.

```go
// non type parameter
func (o Optional) M(t int) {
	...
}

// before
//               ? <- decl ->  usage
//               v     v        v
func (o Optional[T]) M[T any](t T) {
	...
}

// after
// declaration   ->   usage -> usage
//   v                  v       v
func[T any] (o Optional[T]) M(t T) {
	...
}
```

Currently only some type can used as map key, like numbers, string, bool, interface etc.
When I write such type.
```go
type Identity struct {
    Guid string
    Name string
    Tags []string
}
```
I can't use identity as map key, even only field `Guid` is necessary to identify an Identity, just because `Identity` contains not comparable slice field `Tags`. 

If I use `*Identity` as map key, when I load two `*Identity` from different source like `HTTP` and `DB`, it would be not same with each other even if them all have same `Guid`.

## Proposal
So, I propose use `Hashable` or `Comparable[T]` as map key.

For `Identity`
When implements `Hashable`, it becomes
```go
func (i Identity) Hash() uint {
    return hash(i.Guid)
}

var identityMap = map[Identity]bool{
     Identity{
        Guid: "xxx",
        Name: "a",
        Tags: []string{"1", "2"},
    }: false,
    Identity{
        Guid: "xxx",
        Name: "b",
        Tags: []string{"1", "2"},
    }: true, // would raise error
}

```
When implements `Comparable[T]`, is becomes
```go
func (i Identity) Compare(o Identity) bool {
    return i.Guid == o.Guid
}

var identityMap = map[Identity]bool{
     Identity{
        Guid: "xxx",
        Name: "a",
        Tags: []string{"1", "2"},
    }: false,
    Identity{
        Guid: "xxx",
        Name: "b",
        Tags: []string{"1", "2"},
    }: true, // would raise error
}
```

## For existing code
Currently valid map key type auto implements `Hashable` or `Comparable[T]`.

This proposal is a clarification of the original test shuffle flag proposal https://github.com/golang/go/issues/28592

Today I was debugging tests that were rarely failing due to cross-dependency between test functions. To verify the fix I noticed that `go test -shuffle=on -count=100` runs tests in the same order as the first iteration. In other words `-test.shuffle` value is generated once and is reused for the other 99 test runs.

This makes sense when the shuffle seed is set explicitly (`-shuffle=X` ) but for random value (`-shuffle=on`) we should generate a new one to increase test order randomness to cover more combinations.

Current documentation for `count` and `shuffle` flags doesn't mention their relation, so the proposal (if accepted) will not break `go test` behaviour (docs from current `master` branch):

```
//	-count n
//	    Run each test, benchmark, and fuzz seed n times (default 1).
//	    If -cpu is set, run n times for each GOMAXPROCS value.
//	    Examples are always run once. -count does not apply to
//	    fuzz tests matched by -fuzz.
//

...

//
//	-shuffle off,on,N
//	    Randomize the execution order of tests and benchmarks.
//	    It is off by default. If -shuffle is set to on, then it will seed
//	    the randomizer using the system clock. If -shuffle is set to an
//	    integer N, then N will be used as the seed value. In both cases,
//	    the seed will be reported for reproducibility.
//
```

Thank you.

To support [`sendfile` on SectionReader](https://github.com/golang/go/issues/61727) I propose exposing the [`r` and `base` fields of `SectionReader`](https://cs.opensource.google/go/go/+/refs/tags/go1.21.0:src/io/io.go;l=503-504), that would thus become:

```
type SectionReader struct {
    // R is the parent ReaderAt this SectionReader reads from.
	R ReaderAt

    // Base is the offset (relative to R) at which the section starts.
	Base int64

	// unexported fields omitted
}
```

The other two fields (`off` and `limit`) do not need to be exposed (at least for the use-case above) because they can be obtained using `Seek`.

Exposing `Base` as described above will likely require some adjustments to the internal logic to ensure that `Base` is valid every time it is used internally by `SectionReader` (as it may have been modified between calls). As an alternative, `Base() int64` could be a `SectionReader` method returning `r.base`.

I'd like to propose a generic interface to the functionality provided by `strconv`.

``` go
type Types interface {
	string | bool | int | int8 | int16 | int32 | int64 |
		uint | uint8 | uint16 | uint32 | uint64 | uintptr |
		float32 | float64 | complex64 | complex128
}

func Parse[T Types](s string) (T, error)
```

This would mainly be used from other generic functions. For example:

``` go
func QueryValue[T strconv.Types](q url.Values, key string) (T, error) {
	v := q.Get(key)
	if v == "" {
		var z T
		return z, fmt.Errorf("query value not found: %q", key)
	}
	return strconv.Parse[T](v)
}
```

### Proposal Details

Right now as implemented in OpenRaw() it uses a NewSectionReader to return a reader so that it could have a Read, Seek and ReadAt, but for whatever reason it is returning just an io.Reader implementation meaning that with OpenRaw it is not seekable.

Is there a good reason why the function returns `io.Reader` and not `io.NewSectionReader` so we can have more access to the bytes?

It seems like a very easy modification from:
```
func (f *File) OpenRaw() (io.Reader, error) {
	bodyOffset, err := f.findBodyOffset()
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	r := io.NewSectionReader(f.zipr, f.headerOffset+bodyOffset, int64(f.CompressedSize64))
	return r, nil
}
```

to
```
func (f *File) OpenRaw() (io.NewSectionReader, error) {
	bodyOffset, err := f.findBodyOffset()
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	r := io.NewSectionReader(f.zipr, f.headerOffset+bodyOffset, int64(f.CompressedSize64))
	return r, nil
}
```

